  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n- Rain and wind decrease tonight\n\nRain and wind both decrease as we move through tonight. There is a\ndefinite diurnal component to the rain and we see in the models a\ndistinct downward trend in both intensity and areal extent between\n800pm and 1100pm. Mainly dry weather is expected from 1100pm on. \n\nAs for the wind, the peak in the wind will occur this afternoon\nand evening with a noted downward trend in gustiness after 1100pm.\nWind gusts through the evening will reach 20-30 mph with a few\nhigher gusts. After midnight especially winds will decrease\nconsiderably.\n\n- Frost and freeze conditions tonight\n\nWe have gone ahead and issued a Frost Advisory for our southern 2\nrows of counties, essentially from Holland to Lansing to the\nsouth. To the north of there we have issued a Freeze Warning. The\nexception is the U.S. 10 row of counties where the growing season\nhas ended due to earlier freezes. \n\nWe are looking at temperatures tonight ranging from the upper 20s\nnorth in Central Lower Michigan to the lower 30s in the south\ntowards I-94. \n\nFrost and freeze potential may occur in spots again Saturday\nnight. The best chances will be across our southern CWA where\nthere will be less wind.\n\n- Dry weather expected this weekend into this upcoming week\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the area weather both at the surface\nand aloft from this weekend through the upcoming work week.\nEnsemble members of the GFS and the ECWMF are entirely dry during\nthis time frame which leads to a high confidence dry forecast.\n\nHigh temperatures will rebound from the 50s this weekend to the\n60s next week. The normal high for today in Grand Rapids for \nreference is 61. We will have below normal temperatures this\nweekend moderating to slightly above normal next week.",
